  /* The twelve houses: original Greek name, what gets said about the room, what it is from the inside, and a question to grow by. */
  const HNAME = [['Ὡροσκόπος', 'Hōroskopos', 'the Hour-Marker'], ['Ἅιδου πύλη', 'Haidou pylē', 'the Gate of Hades'], ['Θεά', 'Thea', 'the Goddess'], ['Ὑπόγειον', 'Hypogeion', 'Under the Earth'], ['Ἀγαθὴ Τύχη', 'Agathē Tychē', 'Good Fortune'], ['Κακὴ Τύχη', 'Kakē Tychē', 'Bad Fortune'], ['Δύσις', 'Dysis', 'the Setting'], ['Ἀργός', 'Argos', 'the Idle Place'], ['Θεός', 'Theos', 'the God'], ['Μεσουράνημα', 'Mesouranēma', 'the Midheaven'], ['Ἀγαθὸς Δαίμων', 'Agathos Daimōn', 'the Good Spirit'], ['Κακὸς Δαίμων', 'Kakos Daimōn', 'the Bad Spirit']];
  const HSAID = ['your looks and personality', 'money', 'siblings and short trips', 'home and mother', 'romance, children and fun', 'health and the day job', 'marriage and open enemies', 'sex, death and other people’s money', 'travel and higher education', 'career and reputation', 'friends and wishes', 'self-undoing, hidden enemies and confinement'];
  const HREAL = [
    'This is the point where you crossed from not-here to here, and it still describes how you cross every threshold. Jung called the face a person builds for the world the persona. This is the room where that face gets built. The task is to build one you can breathe through: a way of arriving that protects you and still lets the person behind it be seen. Trouble starts when the mask works so well that you forget you are wearing it.',
    'Money is the smallest part of it. This room holds what you can stand on when everything else is taken: your body, your skills, and what you believe you are worth before anyone pays you. The old name fits. This house rises out of the dark just after you do, and it concerns turning raw life into something that sustains a life. A person who doubts their own worth tends to hoard or to give everything away. Growth here looks like a quiet sense of enough.',
    'This is where the mind learns its first language: the names of things, the stories told at your family’s table, and the people who were simply there, brothers, sisters, neighbors. Long before you chose any belief, you inherited a way of noticing. The ancients gave this room to the Moon because it concerns the daily, the familiar and the half-conscious. Growth here is hearing your habits of thought as habits, so that you can ask a real question and take in a real answer.',
    'The lowest point of the chart: midnight, the root. It holds the family you came from as it lives inside you, with its inner mother and father, its unspoken rules and its unfinished business. It is also where you go when you close the door, the private self that no audience sees. Jung thought the second half of life sends everyone back down here. The task is to know your roots well enough to choose which ones you keep feeding.',
    'What comes out of you when nothing is required. Play, flirtation, art and children share this room because they share a root: you make something for the joy of it, and then it has a life of its own. This is the inner child in the serious sense, the part of you that creates before asking whether the result is good enough. A person cut off from this room turns dutiful and dry. Growth here is risking delight, and letting what you make be imperfect and loved.',
    'The old astrologers feared this room because it holds what a person cannot command: the limits of the body, illness, and labor done because it has to be done. Read from the inside, it is the place of craft and upkeep, where a life is kept in working order one ordinary day at a time. The body speaks here when the mind refuses to listen. Growth is learning to serve without servility, and to treat routine as a form of devotion.',
    'Directly across from the self, where the Sun goes down. The qualities you have yet to claim as your own are met here first in other people, which is why this is the room of both the beloved and the open enemy. Jung’s word for it was projection: we fall in love with, or go to war with, a piece of ourselves that someone else is carrying. Partnership is the long work of taking those pieces back, until you can see the actual person across from you.',
    'The room where you are no longer in sole control. Debt, inheritance, intimacy and grief live together here because each one binds you to someone else and changes you without asking permission. The ancients called it idle, since a planet here struggles to act by will alone. This is where the ego learns that it can lose and survive. People avoid the room by never fully merging or by never fully letting go. Growth is being able to do both.',
    'The room where the Sun rejoices: the search for a view large enough to live by. Journeys, teachers, philosophy and faith belong here because each takes you far enough from the familiar to see it whole. The third house inherits a worldview, and the ninth has to earn one. Jung held that the psyche needs meaning the way the body needs food. The risk is borrowed certainty, a doctrine worn like a uniform. Growth is a faith you have tested against your own experience.',
    'The highest point of the chart: noon, where you are most visible. A career is one expression of it. The deeper matter is vocation, the thing your life is trying to accomplish in the world and the authority you grow into by doing it. Everyone’s first picture of authority is a parent, and many people spend years obeying that picture or defying it. Growth is work that answers to something inside you, so that what the world sees and what you are become one thing.',
    'The daimon was the guiding spirit a Greek believed walked beside each person. This room holds the company you choose once family and work have had their say: friends, allies, the causes you join, and the future you allow yourself to hope for. It measures your capacity to receive, which many people find harder than achieving. Growth is finding the people among whom you become more yourself, and letting your hopes become specific.',
    'This room sits just above the horizon, in the stretch of sky the rising self has already left behind, so it falls in a blind spot. The old texts filled it with hidden enemies, exile and confinement. From the inside, it holds what you have been unable to look at so far: old grief, disowned gifts, the life before memory. Jung called this territory the shadow, and Jungians like to say that most of it is gold. What stays unseen here acts on its own and gets called fate. Solitude, dreams and service are the traditional ways in.',
  ];
